title: Your life in data: how much has your country changed since you were born?
subtitle: Enter your birth year and country to see how life there changed over your lifetime — from health and income to democracy and the environment — and how that change compares with the world.
excerpt: Enter your birth year and country to see how life there changed over your lifetime — and how that change compares with the world.
type: article
authors: Mojmir Vinkler

[+body]
Ask people how much has changed in their lifetime and most reach for technology — the phones, the internet. But the deeper changes are the ones that are hard to see from the inside, because they happened to whole countries, one birth at a time.

A woman born in <a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/children-per-woman-un?tab=line&time=1960..latest&country=KOR~OWID_WRL">South Korea in 1960</a> grew up where mothers had six children on average; Korean women today have 0.7, the lowest rate ever recorded anywhere. Someone born in <a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/child-mortality?tab=line&time=1971..latest&country=BGD~OWID_WRL">Bangladesh in 1971</a>, the year it became a country, arrived in a place where more than one child in three died before turning five — today it is closer to one in thirty. And almost everyone born in <a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-of-population-in-extreme-poverty?tab=line&time=1981..latest&country=CHN~OWID_WRL">China in 1981</a> — 97% of the population — lived in extreme poverty. Barely anyone does now.

None of these are famous the way a moon landing or a falling wall is famous. They unfolded slowly, with no single day to point to. But added up over one lifetime in one country, they are among the largest changes in the human condition ever recorded — and almost nobody can tell you the numbers for the place they grew up.

The tool below shows this change for your own life. Enter your birth year and the country you know best, and it builds a “then and now” card: health, income, education, rights, the environment, and more — each shown as the value around the year you were born, the value today, and the line in between. Your country’s path runs against a fainter world line, so you can see whether its story followed the global one or went its own way.

Every number is computed live from the data behind our charts — nothing is typed in by hand — and every row links to the full chart, with its sources and methodology.

{.horizontal-rule}
{}

Pick your country and birth year, then browse the topics: <b>Highlights</b> pulls the most striking changes from every area, and each topic pill goes deeper — you can also swap the comparison line from the world to your continent or income group. Hover a metric for its definition and source, or select it to open the full chart. The address bar updates as you go, so you can send someone born the same year their own card.

{.bespoke-component}
bundle: your-life-in-data
variant: app
size: wide
{.config}
urlSync: true
{}
{}

{.heading}
text: What to look for
level: 2
{}

The most dramatic cards belong to countries that remade themselves within living memory. Take <a href="?lifeCountry=ETH&amp;lifeYear=1975">Ethiopia for someone born in 1975</a>: life expectancy up by 28 years, child mortality down from nearly 30% to under 5%, literacy more than doubled — and much of it faster than the world average. Or <a href="?lifeCountry=CHN&amp;lifeYear=1981&amp;lifeTopic=Economy">China for someone born in 1981</a>, the fastest escape from mass poverty in history. These are whole societies transformed inside a single lifetime.

Some of the biggest shifts aren’t about money at all. <a href="?lifeCountry=ESP&amp;lifeYear=1975&amp;lifeTopic=Rights%20%26%20Democracy">Spain for someone born in 1975</a> arrived in the final months of a dictatorship and grew up as the country became — and stayed — a democracy. <a href="?lifeCountry=KOR&amp;lifeYear=1960&amp;lifeTopic=Population%20%26%20Demography">South Korea for someone born in 1960</a> is a demographic story instead: a country that went from six children per woman to fewer than one, reshaping every generation that comes after.

And the card doesn’t only show progress. Rows that moved against it — rising emissions, worsening obesity — are marked in orange; changes that are neither good nor bad, like a falling birth rate or a growing population, stay grey. The point is an honest summary of what happened where you’re from, not a highlight reel.

{.heading}
text: How the metrics are chosen
level: 2
{}

The hardest question behind a card like this is which of our thousands of indicators earn one of its few rows. We start from a curated spine of around a hundred key metrics across ten topics — the same ones we feature across Our World in Data. That spine is identical for every country, then refined per country: metrics that say little somewhere (tuberculosis in Czechia, say, where it is vanishingly rare) are dropped, and metrics central to a country’s own story are brought forward.

The numbers themselves are never edited. The “then” value is the earliest measurement within your lifetime — shown with its year, since not every series reaches back to every birth year — and the “now” value is the latest published data point. The sparkline covers only the years with data. The world line is context, not a ranking: it’s there so you can tell whether your country’s change was part of a global shift or a story of its own.

{.horizontal-rule}
